input: as5013: restart the poll on resume

as5013_i2c_suspend() cancels the delayed work that polls the chip, with a
comment explaining that i2c is unusable while suspended.  That part is
right.  as5013_i2c_resume() re-enables the interrupt and stops there, so
the poll is never scheduled again.

On a board that wires an interrupt to the chip that is survivable: the
interrupt comes back and the poll was only a fallback.  The Pyra wires
none.  Its device tree gives the as5013 nodes no interrupt gpio, probe
takes the else branch, sets client->irq to 0 and initialises the work, and
from then on the 25 ms poll in as5013_work() is the only thing that ever
reads the joystick.  So the first suspend kills both nubs for good.

enable_irq(0) is a no-op here, which is why the resume path looks like it
is doing something.

Nothing restores the poll except as5013_open(), which reschedules it.
That is why restarting the userspace input daemon appears to repair the
hardware, and why a reboot does: both end in a fresh open.  The chip is
healthy throughout.  It answers on the bus, its control register reads
0xf1, and its axis registers still track the stick.  Nobody is reading
them.

Schedule the work again on resume, guarded by input->users so that a
suspend and resume before anything has opened the device does not start a
poll that the driver would otherwise never have started.

Measured on a Pyra V5.3 by watching the i2c controller the nubs sit on,
which carries nothing else of consequence:

  48072000.i2c interrupts/s   before suspend   450
                              after resume       0
                              after restarting the input daemon  450

With this applied the count is unchanged across suspend and resume.

--- a/drivers/input/misc/as5013.c
+++ b/drivers/input/misc/as5013.c
@@ -836,9 +836,22 @@
 static int as5013_i2c_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct i2c_client *client = to_i2c_client(dev);
+	struct as5013_drvdata *ddata = i2c_get_clientdata(client);
+	struct input_dev *input = ddata->input;
 
 	enable_irq(client->irq);
 
+	/*
+	 * Suspend cancelled the poll. On a board that wires no interrupt to
+	 * this chip the poll is the only thing that ever reads it, and
+	 * nothing else restarts it, so put it back.
+	 */
+	mutex_lock(&input->mutex);
+	if (input->users)
+		schedule_delayed_work(&ddata->work,
+				      msecs_to_jiffies(AS5013_INTERVAL));
+	mutex_unlock(&input->mutex);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 #endif /* CONFIG_PM_SLEEP */
